{
  "id": "m25-crossing-cables",
  "title": "Crossing Cables",
  "source": {
    "name": "Original exercise; classic technique (orientation tests and segment intersection)"
  },
  "rating": 1800,
  "tags": [
    "geometry",
    "cross-product",
    "segment-intersection",
    "orientation",
    "integer-arithmetic"
  ],
  "statement": "`n` straight cables are laid on a flat field; cable `i` runs between the points `(x1, y1)` and `(x2, y2)`. Two cables **touch** if they have at least one point in common. That includes crossing in the middle, meeting at an end, one cable ending on the middle of another, and overlapping along a stretch of the same line.\n\nCount the pairs of cables that touch.\n\nFor example, three cables `(0,0)-(4,4)`, `(0,4)-(4,0)` and `(4,4)-(6,6)` give `2` touching pairs: the first two cross at `(2,2)`, and the first and third meet at the point `(4,4)`; the second and third do not touch.\n\n**Input.** First line: `n`. Then `n` lines `x1 y1 x2 y2`.\n\n**Output.** The number of pairs `i < j` of cables that touch.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 ≤ n ≤ 600 in this browser version (the O(n^2) pairwise test is the idea; a sweep line handles 10^5 segments)",
    "|x|, |y| ≤ 10^6, all coordinates are integers",
    "The two endpoints of a cable are different points; several cables may coincide or overlap"
  ],
  "timeLimitMs": 3000,
