  "hints": [
    "Do not compute the intersection point with division: floating point makes touching cases unreliable. Use the cross product of vectors, which for integer coordinates is exact: cross(b - a, c - a) is positive when c is to the left of the directed line a -> b, negative to the right and zero if collinear.",
    "Segments p1p2 and p3p4 cross properly if p3 and p4 are on opposite sides of line p1p2 and p1 and p2 are on opposite sides of line p3p4 (the signs of the four cross products differ in pairs).",
    "The remaining cases are touching or collinear: if one cross product is zero, that endpoint is on the other segment's line, and it lies on the segment itself exactly when it is inside the bounding box of that segment. Check the four endpoint cases after the proper-crossing test."
  ],
  "editorial": "Count over all `n (n-1) / 2` pairs and test each pair exactly. Let `orient(a, b, c)` be the sign of the cross product `(b - a) x (c - a)`: `+1` if `c` is counter-clockwise of the directed line `a -> b`, `-1` if clockwise and `0` if the three points are collinear. For segments `p1 p2` and `p3 p4` compute `d1 = orient(p3, p4, p1)`, `d2 = orient(p3, p4, p2)`, `d3 = orient(p1, p2, p3)`, `d4 = orient(p1, p2, p4)`. If `d1 * d2 < 0` and `d3 * d4 < 0`, each segment strictly straddles the line through the other, so they **cross properly**. Otherwise they can still touch: if some `d_i = 0`, the corresponding endpoint is collinear with the other segment, and it belongs to that segment exactly when it lies inside the segment's bounding box (a collinear point within the box lies between the endpoints). Test all four endpoints this way; two collinear overlapping segments are caught because one of their endpoints lies on the other. All quantities are integers (cross products are below `8 * 10^12`), so there is no rounding error. The total time is `O(n^2)`.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n^2)",
    "space": "O(n)"
  },
